# NON-FUNCTIONAL STRAWMAN v2 (imagination-tier; whole-cloth, post-sitting; layout
# herein is illustrative -- the renderer owns layout, the semantics engine only
# marks what is critical vs summarizable). One user-moment, two sequential
# commands on one receipt: the 03:40 morning after a survived elision went wrong.
# Exercises: why-as-receipt -- input-identity header -- trust-spent-first /
# problems / improvements -- two-rank marks (`*` runtime-backed vs `!`
# covers-unmeasured: the danger axis is completion-class, not authorship) --
# quoted-speakers rows (speaker first, tier-word as the verb; past tense for run
# events, present tense for standing text) -- `reported` never `measured` (we
# never know what was measured; we know what the author's check reported when it
# ran -- recurring weasel, banked) -- contrastive OUTCOME -- numberless join with
# prose restatement -- endpoint literal excerpt -- suspect/fix/verify handing off
# into `plan --why` (the remediation verb; the goals/consent matrix) -- the
# durable-question float (plan remembers what you asked) -- pure ASCII.

-- web.sh (context, abbreviated) --
 5  apt-get update
 6  dpkg -s nginx >/dev/null 2>&1 || apt-get install -y nginx
 7  cp ./nginx.conf /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
 8  certsync push web1
 9  systemctl enable --now nginx
12  hork tune --profile web
13  ufw allow 443/tcp

-- world (context) --
Cron applied at 02:00 under --risk-faultless-skips. Certs had drifted, so line 8
really ran; certsync's at-most claim omits the systemd drop-in it also rewrites;
line 9's elision survived on that claim; the service came up wrong. The admin
knows only that the site is down and that dorc ran overnight.

-- replay --
$ dorc why
receipt: apply 2026-07-25 02:00:37, host web1, trigger cron
   book web.sh (matches git HEAD 9f31c2e -- unchanged since the run)
   oracles: certsync 1.2, service 2.0, + base library
   risk-profile: --risk-faultless-skips
   plan: 2 ran, 1 guarded, 5 skipped (4 by proof, 1 by trusted claim)
   read top-down; every item is addressable: dorc why <line>

=== TRUST SPENT: skips resting on a human claim, not proof ===

 web.sh:9 | systemctl enable --now nginx
   was skipped PAST 8|certsync really-running, on certsync's at-most claim
   (dorc why web.sh:9)

=== IMPROVEMENTS: dorc can do better, with your help ===

web.sh:12 | hork tune --profile web
   ran blind, as on every apply: hork is undescribed
web.sh:13 | ufw allow 443/tcp
   guarded live instead of skipping: sits past the wall at 12|hork

~2 lines of sh: a convergence check for hork skips lines 12 and 13 whenever hork
is converged                                                  (dorc teach walls)

$ dorc why web.sh:9
   [ web.sh, participating lines:
 8 | certsync push web1
 9 | systemctl enable --now nginx

   === OUTCOME (2026-07-25 02:00:37) ===

   9|systemctl skipped RATHER THAN guarded: 8|certsync ran above it, which
   normally forces an apply-time guard-clause; but --risk-faultless-skips
   activates acting on authors' at-most claims, and certsync's claim does not
   reach what 9|systemctl's skip was proved on.

   === ANALYSIS ===

   9|systemctl being skipped stood on all of the following, together:

   * service.oracle.sh:12  reported  "Package:nginx@{enabled,active}"
                                     (ran 01:59:52, rc 0)
   * service.oracle.sh:9   vouches   "re-running an already-@active unit adds
                                     nothing worth running"
   ! certsync.oracle.sh:31 claims    "`certsync push` touches AT MOST @cert
                                     state"

      This speaks for everything it did NOT measure. Fact derived from
      as-written contract only; no run can back it:

         [ certsync.oracle.sh, as-written:
      30 | # surveyed 2026-05: cert store only.
      31 | push) printf '%s\n' "$1"  : disturbs org.foob.Certs ;;

   so: nothing 8|certsync claims to touch overlaps what 9|systemctl's skip was
   reported on; the skip stood. `!` marks the link that covers unmeasurable
   things; the class of information no runnable command *could* test.

   === NEXT STEPS ===

   if 9|systemctl SHOULD have run:

      suspect: the 8|certsync claim -- the only link speaking for unmeasured
              state. if `certsync push` also touches @enabled/@active, it is
              what wrongly kept 9|systemctl out.

      fix:     certsync.oracle.sh:31 (widen the claim; every consumer heals)

      verify:  `dorc plan --why web.sh web1` -- the fix path: re-measures under
               plan's standing consent, carries this question forward inline,
               and leaves you one step from applying. (plan remembers this
               question: 9|systemctl's fate stays flagged until the analysis
               changes.)

      review:  `dorc why web.sh:9 --all` (every link, unselected, exhaustive, if
               you want)

